ished asset files can be accessed.";s:11:"description";s:69:"The base URL through which the published asset files can be accessed.";s:5:"since";N;s:8:"sinceMap";a:0:{}s:15:"deprecatedSince";N;s:16:"deprecatedReason";N;s:4:"tags";a:1:{i:0;O:43:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Tags\Var_":4:{s:15:"␀*␀variableName";s:0:"";s:7:"␀*␀type";O:38:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\String_":0:{}s:7:"␀*␀name";s:3:"var";s:14:"␀*␀description";O:45:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description":2:{s:59:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀bodyTemplate";s:69:"the base URL through which the published asset files can be accessed.";s:51:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀tags";a:0:{}}}}s:5:"todos";a:0:{}}s:9:"$assetMap";O:29:"yii\apidoc\models\PropertyDoc":22:{s:10:"visibility";s:6:"public";s:8:"isStatic";b:0;s:4:"type";s:8:"string[]";s:5:"types";a:1:{i:0;s:8:"string[]";}s:12:"defaultValue";s:2:"[]";s:6:"getter";N;s:6:"setter";N;s:9:"definedBy";s:20:"yii\web\AssetManager";s:13:"phpDocContext";r:102062;s:4:"name";s:9:"$assetMap";s:8:"fullName";s:31:"yii\web\AssetManager::$assetMap";s:10:"sourceFile";s:95:"/home/mspirkov/open-source/yiisoft/yiiframework.com/data/yii-2.0/framework/web/AssetManager.php";s:9:"startLine";i:124;s:7:"endLine";i:124;s:16:"shortDescription";s:70:"Mapping from source asset files (keys) to target asset files (values).";s:11:"description";s:1134:"Mapping from source asset files (keys) to target asset files (values).

This property is provided to support fixing incorrect asset file paths in some asset bundles.
When an asset bundle is registered with a view, each relative asset file in its [[AssetBundle::css|css]]
and [[AssetBundle::js|js]] arrays will be examined against this map. If any of the keys is found
to be the last part of an asset file (which is prefixed with [[AssetBundle::sourcePath]] if available),
the corresponding value will replace the asset and be registered with the view.
For example, an asset file `my/path/to/jquery.js` matches a key `jquery.js`.

Note that the target asset files should be absolute URLs, domain relative URLs (starting from '/') or paths
relative to [[baseUrl]] and [[basePath]].

In the following example, any assets ending with `jquery.min.js` will be replaced with `jquery/dist/jquery.js`
which is relative to [[baseUrl]] and [[basePath]].

```
[
    'jquery.min.js' => 'jquery/dist/jquery.js',
]
```

You may also use aliases while specifying map value, for example:

```
[
    'jquery.min.js' => '@web/js/jquery/jquery.js',
]
```";s:5:"since";N;s:8:"sinceMap";a:0:{}s:15:"deprecatedSince";N;s:16:"deprecatedReason";N;s:4:"tags";a:1:{i:0;O:43:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Tags\Var_":4:{s:15:"␀*␀variableName";s:0:"";s:7:"␀*␀type";O:37:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\Array_":3:{s:12:"␀*␀valueType";O:38:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\String_":0:{}s:10:"␀*␀keyType";N;s:17:"␀*␀defaultKeyType";O:39:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\Compound":2:{s:52:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\AggregatedType␀types";a:2:{i:0;O:38:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\String_":0:{}i:1;O:38:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\Integer":0:{}}s:52:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\AggregatedType␀token";s:1:"|";}}s:7:"␀*␀name";s:3:"var";s:14:"␀*␀description";O:45:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description":2:{s:59:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀bodyTemplate";s:1134:"mapping from source asset files (keys) to target asset files (values).

This property is provided to support fixing incorrect asset file paths in some asset bundles.
When an asset bundle is registered with a view, each relative asset file in its [[AssetBundle::css|css]]
and [[AssetBundle::js|js]] arrays will be examined against this map. If any of the keys is found
to be the last part of an asset file (which is prefixed with [[AssetBundle::sourcePath]] if available),
the corresponding value will replace the asset and be registered with the view.
For example, an asset file `my/path/to/jquery.js` matches a key `jquery.js`.

Note that the target asset files should be absolute URLs, domain relative URLs (starting from '/') or paths
relative to [[baseUrl]] and [[basePath]].

In the following example, any assets ending with `jquery.min.js` will be replaced with `jquery/dist/jquery.js`
which is relative to [[baseUrl]] and [[basePath]].

```
[
    'jquery.min.js' => 'jquery/dist/jquery.js',
]
```

You may also use aliases while specifying map value, for example:

```
[
    'jquery.min.js' => '@web/js/jquery/jquery.js',
]
```";s:51:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀tags";a:0:{}}}}s:5:"todos";a:0:{}}s:11:"$linkAssets";O:29:"yii\apidoc\models\PropertyDoc":22:{s:10:"visibility";s:6:"public";s:8:"isStatic";b:0;s:4:"type";s:4:"bool";s:5:"types";a:1:{i:0;s:4:"bool";}s:12:"defaultValue";s:5:"false";s:6:"getter";N;s:6:"setter";N;s:9:"definedBy";s:20:"yii\web\AssetManager";s:13:"phpDocContext";r:102062;s:4:"name";s:11:"$linkAssets";s:8:"fullName";s:33:"yii\web\AssetManager::$linkAssets";s:10:"sourceFile";s:95:"/home/mspirkov/open-source/yiisoft/yiiframework.com/data/yii-2.0/framework/web/AssetManager.php";s:9:"startLine";i:142;s:7:"endLine";i:142;s:16:"shortDescription";s:52:"Whether to use symbolic link to publish asset files.";s:11:"description";s:771:"Whether to use symbolic link to publish asset files. Defaults to false, meaning
asset files are copied to [[basePath]]. Using symbolic links has the benefit that the published
assets will always be consistent with the source assets and there is no copy operation required.
This is especially useful during development.

However, there are special requirements for hosting environments in order to use symbolic links.
In particular, symbolic links are supported only on Linux/Unix, and Windows Vista/2008 or greater.

Moreover, some Web servers need to be properly configured so that the linked assets are accessible
to Web users. For example, for Apache Web server, the following configuration directive should be added
for the Web folder:

```
Options FollowSymLinks
```";s:5:"since";N;s:8:"sinceMap";a:0:{}s:15:"deprecatedSince";N;s:16:"deprecatedReason";N;s:4:"tags";a:1:{i:0;O:43:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Tags\Var_":4:{s:15:"␀*␀variableName";s:0:"";s:7:"␀*␀type";O:38:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\Types\Boolean":0:{}s:7:"␀*␀name";s:3:"var";s:14:"␀*␀description";O:45:"phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description":2:{s:59:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀bodyTemplate";s:771:"whether to use symbolic link to publish asset files. Defaults to false, meaning
asset files are copied to [[basePath]]. Using symbolic links has the benefit that the published
assets will always be consistent with the source assets and there is no copy operation required.
This is especially useful during development.

However, there are special requirements for hosting environments in order to use symbolic links.
In particular, symbolic links are supported only on Linux/Unix, and Windows Vista/2008 or greater.

Moreover, some Web servers need to be properly configured so that the linked assets are accessible
to Web users. For example, for Apache Web server, the following configuration directive should be added
for the Web folder:

```
Options FollowSymLinks
```";s:51:"␀phpDocumentor\Reflection\DocBlock\Description␀tags";a:0:{}}}}s:5:"todos";a:0:{}}s:9:"$fileMode";O:29:"yii\apidoc\models\PropertyDoc":22:{s:10:"visibility";s:6:"public";s:8:"isStatic";b:0;s:4:"type";s:8:"int|null";s:5:"types";a:2:{i:0;s:3:"int";i:1;s:4:"null";}s:12:"defaultValue";N;s:6:"getter";N;s:6:"setter";N;s:9:"definedBy";s:20:"yii\web\AssetManager";s:13:"phpDocContext";r:102062;s:4:"name";s:9:"$fileMode";s:8:"fullName";s:31:"yii\web\AssetManager::$fileMode";s:10:"sourceFile";s:95:"/home/mspirkov/open-source/yiisoft/yiiframework.com/data/yii-2.0/framework/web/AssetManager.php";s:9:"startLine";i:148;s:7:"endLine";i:148;s:16:"shortDescription";s:57:"The permission to be set for newly published asset files.";s:11:"description";s:206:"The permission to be set for newly published asset files.
